.TH TKOFFTEST 1
.SH NAME
tkofftest \- render Tk widgets offscreen and check what they drew
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tkofftest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tkofftest
draws Tk widgets into an image of its own and reads the pixels back,
which cannot be done on the screen: under the Metal present path
.IR draw (2)'s
.B readpixels
returns
.B "00 00 00"
both for a window on the display's screen and for the display image
itself, so what a widget put on the page is invisible to the program that
drew it.
.PP
A Tk toplevel need not be given the display's screen.
.I Tkofftest
allocates a
.B Screen
over an ordinary image, makes a window in it, and hands the toplevel that
window under the request id
.IR tkclient (2)
uses when there is no window manager,
.BR "-1" .
Tk then draws into memory.
The image must be handed over
.I after
the widgets exist: Tk paints what it has when the image arrives.
.PP
These are checked.
.TP
.B "a blank pane reads back blank"
An untouched pane must read back as one value and no ink.
Without this the other checks cannot tell "the widget drew" from "the
readback returns rubbish that happens to vary".
.TP
.B "a widget's own pixels read back"
A label on the same pane must produce more than one value.
.TP
.B "every widget grows with the font"
A label, button, checkbutton, radiobutton, menubutton and scale are
packed at
.B /fonts/lucidasans/unicode.8.font
(15 pixels high) and at
.B /fonts/pelm/ascii.16.font
(30), and every one must grow.
This is the question of whether the toolkit measures its font or assumes a
character cell, which is what decides how much of the window system a font
change would break.
Widgets whose size is configured in character units instead \(em an
.BR entry 's
.BR -width ,
a
.BR text 's
.B -width
and
.B -height
\(em are deliberately not in that list, because their size is meant not to
follow the font.
.TP
.B "the titlebar holds together"
The real
.IR titlebar (2)
is built on the offscreen toplevel at both fonts and measured: the title
must not run into the buttons and the bar must stay inside the pane.
No font size makes this fail, because Tk's packer clips the title \(em at
the taller font it ends at 631 pixels against buttons at 820, and forcing
its width to 2000 only takes it to 799.
It guards against the titlebar being given fixed geometry, not against a
font change.
.TP
.B "a window cannot be shorter than its titlebar"
.B titlebar->minsize
is the smallest a window may be resized to.
It combined only the button rects, which are fixed-size bitmaps, so it
came up short by however much the title's text is taller: one pixel at the
tree's own font and twelve at a 32-pixel one.
This check fails at both fonts without that fix.
.TP
.B "a real (tkclient) window inherits the same default font"
This test builds its toplevels with
.BR tk->toplevel ;
every actual program builds one with
.BR tkclient->toplevel ,
which connects to a window manager and puts a titlebar on before it
reaches a widget.
If anything on that path named a font of its own, the default set by
.IR emu (1)'s
.B -f
would reach a bare toplevel and miss every real window.
The check asks both and requires the same answer.
.TP
.B "the default TrueType face is antialiased in a real window"
Only when the default
.I is
a face under
.BR /mnt/ft ,
that is, under
.BR "emu -f" .
Named alone proves nothing; this reads the window's pixels back.
The bitmap default gives 3 distinct values,
.B /mnt/ft/go-mono.r.13.font
gives 199.
When the default is a bitmap face the count is printed rather than
asserted, since 3 is then the right answer.
.TP
.B "the layout checker names widgets that lost room"
A positive control for
.IR tklayout (2):
the same two widgets are packed into a pane far too small for them, and
both must be named with the room they got and the room they needed.
A checker that has never been seen to report anything has not been shown
to look at anything, and this one twice reported nothing while broken \(em
once because the control used
.BR place ,
which this
.B Tk
does not have, and once because a filter added to suppress false
positives suppressed the real detection too.
.TP
.B "the same widgets with room to spare come back clean"
The other half of that control.
Without it, a checker that simply always complains would pass.
.TP
.B "no titlebar widget is short of room"
The chrome every window has, at both fonts, through
.IR tklayout (2).
.TP
.B "every widget that takes the keyboard shows it"
Each of the seven widget types carrying
.B Tktakefocus
is built twice on one pane, the keyboard is given to one of the pair and
then the other, and the widget's outer four pixels are compared between
the two readings.
.PP
Counting ink over the WHOLE widget was not enough, and the difference is
the point of the check.
A
.B text
widget answered that version with its blinking caret \(em ten pixels, in
the middle of the widget, absent for half of every second \(em and the
check said it was fine.
Requiring the change to be at the edge, and to amount to a fair part of a
perimeter, is what turns "something moved" into "the widget is visibly
the one with the keyboard".
.PP
It found three widgets showing nothing at all:
.IR listbox (9)
used the keyboard only to pick out its active
.IR item ,
.IR scale (9)
had ring-drawing code that could never draw because it never reserved a
highlight pixel, and
.IR text (9)
had only the caret.
The same metric then caught the first repair of the last of those putting
its ring one border-width too far in, where it covered half the perimeter
rather than all of it.
.TP
.B "Tk antialiases a TrueType face"
Draws through the first face
.IR ttffs (4)
is serving, at 16 pixels.
A bitmap font can put only two values on the page;
.B /mnt/ft/go.r.16.font
puts 185 \(em the same number
.IR aadrawtest (1)
arrives at by scanning a tight box around one string rather than a whole
pane.
Skipped when nothing is served.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
	tkofftest
.EE
.PP
The faces this tree ships are in
.BR /fonts/ttf ;
.I tkofftest
starts
.IR ttffs (4)
itself if nothing is serving them, and stops it again before it returns,
since a Styx server's loop does not end and
.I emu
waits for every process.
To see the whole chain instead, name a face as the default:
.EX
	emu -f /mnt/ft/go-mono.r.13.font /dis/tkofftest.dis
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/tkofftest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tk (2),
.IR tkclient (2),
.IR draw (2),
.IR ttffs (4),
.IR tklayout (2),
.B /doc/ui-modernization-plan.md
.SH BUGS
It checks that widgets are sized by the font, not that they are
.I placed
correctly: nothing here would catch two widgets drawn on top of each
other, which is the failure a font change actually causes.
The recording check that would \(em every text box, tested for overlap and
for leaving the pane \(em exists only inside the temple ports and has not
been made general.
.PP
Only the packing of one flat dialog is exercised; no menu, canvas or text
widget is drawn at all.
.PP
The layout checks here are of
.IR tklayout (2)
itself, on two widgets and on the titlebar.
Sweeping real programs with it is a separate exercise, driven through
.B $tklayout
and
.IR tkclient (2);
nothing in this test does that.
.PP
The antialiasing checks are skipped silently rather than failing when no
face is being served, so a green run does not by itself mean that path was
tested \(em read the reported font name.
.PP
The real-window check builds a
.IR tkclient (2)
toplevel with no window manager behind it.
That is the configuration
.B ". -1"
exists for, but it is not the configuration a program runs in under
.IR wm (1),
and no check here covers the difference.
.PP
The render-node comparison never presses anything.
A scrollbar arrow or slider held down with the mouse is drawn in the
accent colour, the same shape as the unpressed one, and that colour is
not compared between the two paths: pressing starts the scrollbar's
autorepeat and runs its command, which this test does not set up.
